1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,379
UltraQuant: one pass over a four bit KV cache, for speculative decoding, on AMD, NVIDIA and

2
00:00:06,379 --> 00:00:07,102
Intel GPUs.

3
00:00:07,102 --> 00:00:12,490
This is a short tour of what it does, what we measured, and where it does not win.

4
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:16,619
Speculative decoding verifies K draft tokens in one step.

5
00:00:16,619 --> 00:00:21,834
A straightforward kernel handles each of the K queries separately, so each one reads the entire

6
00:00:21,834 --> 00:00:22,657
KV cache again.

7
00:00:22,657 --> 00:00:26,171
Decode is memory bound, so that costs close to K times the time.

8
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:28,782
The idea is to fuse them.

9
00:00:28,782 --> 00:00:34,586
All K queries, and the query heads that share a key value head, are stacked into one tile.

10
00:00:34,586 --> 00:00:39,293
Each page of the cache is read once, and all K outputs come out together.

11
00:00:39,293 --> 00:00:40,712
On the left, K passes.

12
00:00:40,712 --> 00:00:41,873
On the right, one.

13
00:00:42,870 --> 00:00:44,947
The cache is stored in four bits.

14
00:00:44,947 --> 00:00:50,801
Each value is an FP4 E2M1 float, and every thirty two values share one eight bit power of two

15
00:00:50,801 --> 00:00:52,060
scale, called UE8M0.

16
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:57,852
At head dimension one twenty eight that is 68 bytes per head per token, against 256 for half

17
00:00:57,852 --> 00:00:59,488
precision and 128 for fp8.

18
00:00:59,488 --> 00:01:02,447
That is 3.76 times smaller than half precision.

19
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:06,179
Inside the kernel, the queries form one tile.

20
00:01:06,179 --> 00:01:11,879
FP4 pages are expanded to bf16 in registers, each query gets its own causal mask, and split KV

21
00:01:11,879 --> 00:01:15,275
partial results are merged with a log sum exp reduction.

22
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:22,826
The same operation runs on three vendors: a Triton kernel on AMD MI300X, native CUDA on five

23
00:01:22,826 --> 00:01:27,238
NVIDIA GPUs, and SYCL with XMX and ESIMD on an Intel Arc B390.

24
00:01:27,238 --> 00:01:33,571
All three read the same cache layout, a pure permute of the upstream one, and are checked

25
00:01:33,571 --> 00:01:39,761
against the same CPU reference vectors: 8,228 CUDA launches and 36 SYCL runs, with zero

26
00:01:39,761 --> 00:01:40,402
failures.

27
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:47,920
Time for one attention layer, batch eight, thirty two thousand tokens of context.

28
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:52,348
Going from one speculative token to eight costs little.

29
00:01:52,348 --> 00:01:56,292
On MI300X it goes from 0.36 to 0.54 milliseconds.

30
00:01:56,292 --> 00:01:58,466
On H100, from 0.16 to 0.22.

31
00:01:58,466 --> 00:02:00,639
On B300, from 0.14 to 0.31.

32
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:08,528
Against running the same kernel K times, the fused kernel at K equals eight is 4.1 times faster

33
00:02:08,528 --> 00:02:12,298
on MI300X, 5.6 times on H100, and 3.3 times on B300.

34
00:02:12,298 --> 00:02:17,735
Across all six GPUs, the gain at K equals eight runs from 2.5 to 5.6 times.

35
00:02:17,735 --> 00:02:20,708
At K equals one there is nothing to fuse.

36
00:02:21,710 --> 00:02:24,128
How does it compare with other schemes?

37
00:02:24,128 --> 00:02:30,019
We ported TurboQuant, NVFP4, FP8, KIVI, QServe, KVQuant, SpectralQuant, and the Planar, Iso and

38
00:02:30,019 --> 00:02:32,747
Rotor quantisers to the same MI300X harness.

39
00:02:32,747 --> 00:02:38,452
Speed is horizontal, perplexity increase on Qwen three eight B is vertical, so lower left is

40
00:02:38,452 --> 00:02:38,886
better.

41
00:02:38,886 --> 00:02:44,218
UltraQuant beats TurboQuant, SpectralQuant and the three bit rotation methods on both.

42
00:02:44,218 --> 00:02:46,203
But it is not the most accurate.

43
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:50,277
On accuracy, UltraQuant is not the leader.

44
00:02:50,277 --> 00:02:56,431
QServe int four at the same sixty eight bytes, NVFP4, FP8 and KIVI four all beat it.

45
00:02:56,431 --> 00:03:02,878
NVFP4, for four more bytes, cuts the Qwen three perplexity increase from 0.562 to 0.081.

46
00:03:02,878 --> 00:03:05,443
FP8 is also faster at K equals one.

47
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:08,934
On B300, a vendor kernel is faster.

48
00:03:08,934 --> 00:03:15,631
FlashInfer's NVFP4 decode, which also shares the read across queries, takes 0.066 milliseconds

49
00:03:15,631 --> 00:03:18,481
at K equals one, against 0.139 for ours.

50
00:03:18,481 --> 00:03:20,761
That is 2.1 to 3.3 times faster.

51
00:03:20,761 --> 00:03:23,753
We do not beat vendor tuned kernels there.

52
00:03:24,750 --> 00:03:25,903
In a real server?

53
00:03:25,903 --> 00:03:32,209
We ran vLLM's own native four bit backend, not our kernel, with Qwen three eight B on MI300X.

54
00:03:32,209 --> 00:03:38,516
KV capacity rises from 1.09 million tokens to 3.16 million, which is 2.89 times, because vLLM

55
00:03:38,516 --> 00:03:40,957
keeps four layers at full precision.

56
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:43,543
The costs are real.

57
00:03:43,543 --> 00:03:47,791
Question answering F one drops from 0.655 to 0.618.

58
00:03:47,791 --> 00:03:55,371
Greedy output diverges from bf sixteen after a median of 10 tokens, earlier than fp8 at 24.

59
00:03:55,371 --> 00:04:00,286
Plain decode is slower: 767 tokens per second against 1052.

60
00:04:00,286 --> 00:04:06,451
In an oversubscribed agent test FP4 wins, 59 against 48 tokens per second.

61
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:08,492
Honest caveats.

62
00:04:08,492 --> 00:04:11,966
Most kernel numbers are synthetic microbenchmarks.

63
00:04:11,966 --> 00:04:18,566
The serving results come from vLLM's native backend, not our kernel, as single runs on a shared

64
00:04:18,566 --> 00:04:18,844
GPU.

65
00:04:18,844 --> 00:04:24,750
MI300X and Arc have no native FP4 matrix instructions, so FP4 is decoded in software.

66
00:04:24,750 --> 00:04:27,876
And on B300, vendor NVFP4 kernels are faster.

67
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:30,188
So, is it the best?

68
00:04:30,188 --> 00:04:30,394
No.

69
00:04:30,394 --> 00:04:31,908
Here is where it wins.

70
00:04:31,908 --> 00:04:35,900
One validated implementation across AMD, NVIDIA and Intel.

71
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:40,650
A fused K query pass that is 2.5 to 5.6 times faster than K launches.

72
00:04:40,650 --> 00:04:43,609
And 3.76 times more KV tokens per gigabyte.

73
00:04:43,609 --> 00:04:49,804
In the public sources we checked, we found no public CUDA or SYCL port of this layout with

74
00:04:49,804 --> 00:04:51,249
fused K query verify.

75
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:56,527
The code, the results, and the blog post are all in the repo.

